WEBVTT

00:00:06.924 --> 00:00:08.133
[static crackling]

00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:12.721
[static crackling]

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:15.766
[indistinct muffled radio chatter]

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.897
[David Attenborough] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:22.481 --> 00:00:25.400
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:39.373 --> 00:00:44.878
<i>For the very first time,</i>
<i>we look back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:57.766
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:03.272 --> 00:01:07.818
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain,</i>

00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:11.363
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:15.993
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:38.265
<i>When human beings</i>
<i>built their first settlements</i>

00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:40.475
<i>some 10,000 years ago,</i>

00:01:41.268 --> 00:01:42.519
<i>the world around them,</i>

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:45.522
<i>on the land and in the sea,</i>

00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:47.149
<i>was full of life.</i>

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:11.340
<i>For generations, this stable Eden</i>
<i>nurtured our growing civilizations.</i>

00:02:16.011 --> 00:02:20.265
<i>But now, in the space</i>
<i>of just one human lifetime,</i>

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:22.768
<i>all that has changed.</i>

00:02:26.355 --> 00:02:27.939
<i>In the last 50 years,</i>

00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:32.944
<i>wildlife populations have, on average,</i>
<i>declined by 60 percent.</i>

00:02:36.323 --> 00:02:38.659
<i>For the first time in human history,</i>

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:43.497
<i>the stability of nature</i>
<i>can no longer be taken for granted.</i>

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:45.499
[ice cracking]

00:02:55.133 --> 00:02:57.969
<i>But the natural world is resilient.</i>

00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:01.598
<i>Great riches still remain.</i>

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:11.983
<i>And with our help, the planet can recover.</i>

00:03:16.238 --> 00:03:21.993
<i>Never has it been more important</i>
<i>to understand how the natural world works,</i>

00:03:22.703 --> 00:03:24.079
<i>and how to help it.</i>

00:03:36.842 --> 00:03:40.095
[birds calling]

00:03:43.515 --> 00:03:45.517
[waves crashing]

00:03:46.727 --> 00:03:50.230
<i>Wildlife still flourishes</i>
<i>in astonishing numbers</i>

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:53.108
<i>in a few precious places.</i>

00:03:59.114 --> 00:04:01.908
<i>Along the Peruvian coast of South America,</i>

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:07.038
<i>seabirds congregate</i>
<i>in colonies millions strong.</i>

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:13.503
<i>They come here to breed.</i>

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:22.679
[birds calling]

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.808
<i>Every morning,</i>
<i>the birds leave their colonies</i>

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.062
<i>to fish in one of the richest seas</i>
<i>on Earth.</i>

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:44.201
<i>It is an astonishing daily migration</i>

00:04:44.826 --> 00:04:47.287
<i>of five million birds.</i>

00:04:52.209 --> 00:04:58.131
<i>The huge flocks of cormorants and boobies</i>
<i>are all seeking one thing:</i>

00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:02.594
<i>anchovies.</i>

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:07.766
[water rushing]

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.944
<i>The boobies carpet-bomb the shoals.</i>

00:05:27.786 --> 00:05:31.540
<i>More and more birds</i>
<i>join the feeding frenzy.</i>

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:33.750
[water splashing]

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:35.377
[classical music score plays]

00:06:11.997 --> 00:06:15.750
<i>All in this immense assembly are here</i>

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:19.963
<i>because a powerful oceanic current,</i>
<i>the Humboldt,</i>

00:06:20.255 --> 00:06:22.090
<i>sweeps up from the Antarctic,</i>

00:06:22.841 --> 00:06:26.761
<i>bringing with it rich nutrients</i>
<i>from the ocean's depths.</i>

00:06:31.975 --> 00:06:35.312
<i>90 percent of the life in the oceans</i>

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.732
<i>is found in the shallow seas</i>
<i>close to the coast.</i>

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:44.821
<i>Away from the land,</i>

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.909
<i>the seas, for the most part,</i>
<i>are a blue desert.</i>

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:53.830
<i>But even these distant waters</i>
<i>may be enriched</i>

00:06:53.914 --> 00:06:57.667
<i>by a most unexpected connection</i>
<i>to the land.</i>

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:04.424
<i>Some deserts,</i>

00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:07.135
<i>often hundreds of kilometers</i>
<i>from the ocean,</i>

00:07:07.594 --> 00:07:11.264
<i>provide the raw materials for life.</i>

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:14.976
[wind blowing]

00:07:21.775 --> 00:07:28.573
<i>Every year, winds sweep up</i>
<i>two billion tons of dust into the sky.</i>

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:35.580
<i>At least a quarter of it</i>
<i>eventually falls on the sea,</i>

00:07:36.498 --> 00:07:40.752
<i>providing nutrients</i>
<i>needed by the microscopic organisms</i>

00:07:41.086 --> 00:07:44.422
<i>that are the foundations of ocean life.</i>

00:07:46.841 --> 00:07:48.551
[water splashing]

00:07:55.058 --> 00:07:58.019
<i>Dolphins explore the vast, open ocean</i>

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:03.066
<i>in search of the riches</i>
<i>that distant deserts may have nourished.</i>

00:08:05.777 --> 00:08:07.362
[dolphins spouting]

00:08:12.283 --> 00:08:16.579
<i>A shoal of mackerel</i>
<i>has discovered a swarm of krill...</i>

00:08:18.415 --> 00:08:20.125
<i>the small crustaceans</i>

00:08:20.208 --> 00:08:23.545
<i>that feed on the ocean's</i>
<i>floating microscopic plants.</i>

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.967
<i>But the mackerel themselves</i>
<i>are food for the dolphins.</i>

00:08:29.384 --> 00:08:31.553
[dolphins whistling]

00:08:39.394 --> 00:08:41.896
[water rushing]

00:08:45.108 --> 00:08:48.153
<i>They drive the mackerel</i>
<i>towards the surface,</i>

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:51.197
<i>and into the range of birds.</i>

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.035
<i>Shearwaters.</i>

00:08:56.828 --> 00:08:59.748
<i>The wings that normally propel the birds</i>
<i>through the air</i>

00:08:59.831 --> 00:09:03.209
<i>now drive them six meters down</i>
<i>through the water.</i>

00:09:03.293 --> 00:09:04.794
[water rushing]

00:09:10.717 --> 00:09:13.219
<i>Whilst the birds</i>
<i>pick off the top of the shoal...</i>

00:09:17.098 --> 00:09:19.684
<i>the dolphins attack the underside.</i>

00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:31.488
[water rushing]

00:09:31.571 --> 00:09:33.823
[dolphins whistling]

00:09:49.631 --> 00:09:51.424
[water rushing]

00:09:55.178 --> 00:09:57.263
<i>After 20 minutes of feasting,</i>

00:09:58.181 --> 00:10:00.975
<i>the predators</i>
<i>from both the sea and the air</i>

00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:03.269
<i>have had their fill.</i>

00:10:21.037 --> 00:10:23.832
<i>The stability of life on our planet</i>

00:10:24.082 --> 00:10:28.419
<i>relies on such connections</i>
<i>between different habitats.</i>

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:34.717
<i>Water evaporating</i>
<i>from the surface of the sea</i>

00:10:35.218 --> 00:10:38.138
<i>condenses to form great clouds.</i>

00:10:40.849 --> 00:10:46.688
<i>And these eventually</i>
<i>release the fresh water as rain.</i>

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:56.531
<i>But these life-giving rains</i>
<i>are not evenly spread over the land.</i>

00:11:04.038 --> 00:11:06.749
<i>This vast salt pan in Africa</i>

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:10.503
<i>is all that remains of an ancient lake.</i>

00:11:14.215 --> 00:11:17.552
<i>It's totally waterless and oven-hot.</i>

00:11:18.636 --> 00:11:22.265
<i>Few places on the land</i>
<i>are more hostile to life.</i>

00:11:29.063 --> 00:11:35.278
<i>A few tracks cross it, made by animals</i>
<i>searching unsuccessfully for water.</i>

00:11:41.868 --> 00:11:42.952
[snorting]

00:11:55.590 --> 00:12:01.596
<i>But very occasionally,</i>
<i>this whole landscape is transformed.</i>

00:12:05.475 --> 00:12:07.227
[thunder rumbling]

00:12:15.235 --> 00:12:16.569
[thunderclap]

00:12:20.531 --> 00:12:24.327
<i>A huge deluge drenches the salt pan.</i>

00:12:26.788 --> 00:12:28.539
[rain pouring]

00:12:28.957 --> 00:12:30.124
[thunder rumbling]

00:12:40.385 --> 00:12:42.845
<i>Triggered by some unknown signal,</i>

00:12:43.221 --> 00:12:48.726
<i>flocks of lesser flamingos</i>
<i>arrive from thousands of kilometers away.</i>

00:12:59.612 --> 00:13:02.240
<i>The algae that the flamingos feed on</i>

00:13:02.573 --> 00:13:05.868
<i>have lain dormant as spores in the dust.</i>

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.500
<i>But most importantly,</i>

00:13:13.001 --> 00:13:15.503
<i>the birds are here to breed.</i>

00:13:17.922 --> 00:13:22.760
<i>Perfect conditions might occur</i>
<i>only once in a decade.</i>

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:28.933
[flamingos squawking]

00:13:29.017 --> 00:13:32.103
<i>The birds nest on an island</i>
<i>far from the shore.</i>

00:13:32.854 --> 00:13:34.772
[mud splashing]

00:13:34.856 --> 00:13:38.026
<i>They build mounds of mud</i>
<i>that raise up their eggs</i>

00:13:38.109 --> 00:13:43.031
<i>and so keep them just marginally cooler</i>
<i>than they would be at ground level.</i>

00:13:43.114 --> 00:13:44.741
[flamingos chattering]

00:13:47.243 --> 00:13:50.204
<i>The water surrounding the island</i>
<i>is so salty</i>

00:13:50.288 --> 00:13:52.790
<i>that predators do not venture into it.</i>

00:13:53.875 --> 00:13:55.835
<i>So the nests are safe.</i>

00:13:55.918 --> 00:13:57.420
[squawking]

00:14:00.298 --> 00:14:05.178
<i>Thirty days later,</i>
<i>thousands of chicks start to hatch.</i>

00:14:18.649 --> 00:14:22.570
<i>But there is no shelter</i>
<i>from the scorching sun.</i>

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:29.160
<i>The water that once surrounded</i>
<i>their island, protecting them,</i>

00:14:29.494 --> 00:14:31.245
<i>has now dried up altogether.</i>

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.586
[cheeping]

00:14:38.669 --> 00:14:43.800
<i>The last to hatch</i>
<i>step out into a desperately harsh world.</i>

00:14:46.469 --> 00:14:48.554
-[chicks cheeping]
-[squawking]

00:14:55.436 --> 00:14:56.521
[squawking]

00:14:57.397 --> 00:15:02.568
<i>Somehow or other, the growing chicks</i>
<i>must find fresh water to drink.</i>

00:15:03.486 --> 00:15:05.154
-[cheeping]
-[squawking]

00:15:07.115 --> 00:15:12.412
<i>They cannot yet fly, so they must walk,</i>
<i>guided by some of the adults.</i>

00:15:26.759 --> 00:15:30.012
<i>They may have to trek for 50 kilometers.</i>

00:15:55.246 --> 00:15:56.956
[frenzied cheeping and squawking]

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:03.963
<i>Some... cannot keep up.</i>

00:16:09.594 --> 00:16:12.889
<i>The salt has solidified around their legs.</i>

00:16:13.723 --> 00:16:14.640
[cheeps]

00:16:44.879 --> 00:16:46.672
-[squawking]
-[splashing]

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:52.094
<i>Most of the chicks,</i>
<i>in spite of everything,</i>

00:16:52.428 --> 00:16:54.263
<i>and having walked for days,</i>

00:16:54.347 --> 00:16:56.724
<i>eventually reach fresh water.</i>

00:17:03.189 --> 00:17:07.443
[frenzied squawking]

00:17:17.453 --> 00:17:19.539
<i>It is the end of a long journey...</i>

00:17:21.457 --> 00:17:26.295
<i>but only the first of the trials</i>
<i>that will be imposed on these flamingos</i>

00:17:26.462 --> 00:17:29.048
<i>by the irregularity of the rains.</i>

00:17:33.594 --> 00:17:36.097
-[rainfall]
-[snorting]

00:17:39.308 --> 00:17:42.436
<i>If rainfall is more predictable</i>
<i>and certain,</i>

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:45.189
<i>then life can flourish more richly,</i>

00:17:46.315 --> 00:17:49.569
<i>both in numbers and variety.</i>

00:17:53.823 --> 00:17:59.453
<i>The Serengeti plains in East Africa</i>
<i>support over a million wildebeest.</i>

00:17:59.829 --> 00:18:01.998
[grunting]

00:18:02.456 --> 00:18:05.293
<i>The herds follow the seasonal rains,</i>

00:18:05.585 --> 00:18:09.422
<i>grazing on the newly-sprouting grass</i>
<i>that comes in their wake.</i>

00:18:09.797 --> 00:18:11.966
[grunting continues]

00:18:20.182 --> 00:18:22.727
<i>Each year, within a three-week period,</i>

00:18:23.019 --> 00:18:27.773
<i>the females give birth</i>
<i>to over a quarter of a million calves.</i>

00:18:34.989 --> 00:18:36.824
[bleating]

00:18:36.907 --> 00:18:39.744
<i>This youngster is just a few days old.</i>

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:45.124
<i>Playing strengthens its legs</i>
<i>for the long journey that lies ahead.</i>

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:57.762
[grunting]

00:19:16.697 --> 00:19:19.283
<i>The calf must stay close to its mother.</i>

00:19:19.825 --> 00:19:22.203
<i>Without her milk, it would starve.</i>

00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:24.580
-[snorting]
-[grunting]

00:19:26.415 --> 00:19:28.959
<i>And the herds are always traveling,</i>

00:19:30.086 --> 00:19:33.172
<i>following the rains</i>
<i>as they drift across the plains</i>

00:19:33.881 --> 00:19:35.800
<i>in order to find fresh grazing.</i>

00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:43.265
<i>Eventually, they reach woodlands.</i>

00:19:43.683 --> 00:19:46.811
[birds chirping]

00:19:46.894 --> 00:19:48.229
[grunting]

00:20:02.159 --> 00:20:03.661
<i>Hunting dogs.</i>

00:20:08.165 --> 00:20:11.168
<i>Wildebeest calves are a favorite prey.</i>

00:20:15.548 --> 00:20:17.383
<i>And the dogs are hungry.</i>

00:20:32.064 --> 00:20:36.485
<i>The calf must stay with its mother,</i>
<i>protected within the herd.</i>

00:21:07.683 --> 00:21:09.226
[wildebeest snorting]

00:21:19.653 --> 00:21:22.031
<i>The dogs have incredible stamina...</i>

00:21:25.993 --> 00:21:28.704
<i>but the calf is defended by the herd.</i>

00:21:37.505 --> 00:21:39.632
<i>They need the calf on its own.</i>

00:21:40.841 --> 00:21:42.051
[snorting]

00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:44.762
[African wild dogs panting]

00:21:58.275 --> 00:22:01.987
<i>The mother blocks the dogs,</i>
<i>shielding her calf.</i>

00:22:09.662 --> 00:22:11.622
<i>It makes a run for safety.</i>

00:22:22.633 --> 00:22:25.761
<i>And it just manages</i>
<i>to get back to the herd.</i>

00:22:52.496 --> 00:22:58.419
<i>The future of this whole migration</i>
<i>depends on the regularity of the rains,</i>

00:22:59.378 --> 00:23:04.133
<i>but also on the continued existence</i>
<i>of the great open grasslands</i>

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:07.845
<i>across which the herds</i>
<i>make their immense journeys.</i>

00:23:11.015 --> 00:23:13.183
-[insects chittering]
-[birds calling]

00:23:15.603 --> 00:23:21.609
<i>In places where rains fall abundantly</i>
<i>throughout the year, forests grow,</i>

00:23:22.902 --> 00:23:25.070
<i>and in the warmth of the tropics,</i>

00:23:25.154 --> 00:23:28.574
<i>they support</i>
<i>an unparalleled richness of life.</i>

00:23:28.657 --> 00:23:31.952
[overlapping animal vocalizations]

00:23:32.036 --> 00:23:35.205
<i>Half of all the species</i>
<i>of land-living animals</i>

00:23:35.289 --> 00:23:37.666
<i>live in these stable worlds.</i>

00:23:39.543 --> 00:23:41.253
[bird whistling]

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:44.715
<i>The sheer diversity is breathtaking.</i>

00:23:50.679 --> 00:23:52.640
<i>We still have not catalogued</i>

00:23:52.723 --> 00:23:56.143
<i>all the species that live</i>
<i>in the tropical forests.</i>

00:24:10.699 --> 00:24:16.622
<i>The relationships between them all</i>
<i>are multitudinous and complex.</i>

00:24:20.709 --> 00:24:22.169
[wings buzzing]

00:24:22.252 --> 00:24:26.340
<i>Plants often depend on animals</i>
<i>to pollinate their flowers.</i>

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:31.929
<i>And these intimate connections</i>

00:24:32.012 --> 00:24:35.683
<i>are just as important</i>
<i>as the great global ones.</i>

00:24:35.766 --> 00:24:37.434
[buzzing]

00:24:45.442 --> 00:24:47.277
<i>These are traps.</i>

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:50.739
<i>Flowers shaped like buckets,</i>

00:24:51.407 --> 00:24:52.783
<i>produced by an orchid.</i>

00:24:58.497 --> 00:24:59.790
[buzzing]

00:25:02.126 --> 00:25:07.256
<i>Each red bucket is filled</i>
<i>with an oily liquid that drips from above.</i>

00:25:12.928 --> 00:25:18.851
<i>Male orchid bees need a rich perfume</i>
<i>with which to impress their females,</i>

00:25:20.352 --> 00:25:22.563
<i>and the orchids provide it.</i>

00:25:25.941 --> 00:25:28.986
<i>But the bucket is slippery,</i>

00:25:31.989 --> 00:25:35.534
<i>and the liquid</i>
<i>into which the bee has fallen is sticky.</i>

00:25:39.455 --> 00:25:40.998
<i>The only way to get out</i>

00:25:42.416 --> 00:25:44.168
<i>is through a narrow tunnel.</i>

00:25:47.629 --> 00:25:51.008
<i>As it emerges, the bee is gripped tight.</i>

00:25:53.677 --> 00:25:56.180
<i>And that gives enough time for the plant</i>

00:25:56.930 --> 00:26:00.184
<i>to glue pollen sacs on the bee's back.</i>

00:26:03.729 --> 00:26:08.150
<i>So the orchid has its pollen</i>
<i>taken to another plant...</i>

00:26:10.611 --> 00:26:13.155
<i>and the bee is rewarded with a perfume,</i>

00:26:13.363 --> 00:26:16.116
<i>with which, when it recovers its strength,</i>

00:26:16.325 --> 00:26:18.202
<i>it can woo a female.</i>

00:26:22.831 --> 00:26:24.374
[water crashing]

00:26:25.626 --> 00:26:29.171
<i>There are no pronounced seasons</i>
<i>in a rainforest.</i>

00:26:32.674 --> 00:26:37.012
<i>It produces food in one form or another</i>
<i>the year round.</i>

00:26:41.141 --> 00:26:44.353
<i>It's so rich</i>
<i>that the females of some birds</i>

00:26:44.436 --> 00:26:47.856
<i>are able to raise their young</i>
<i>entirely by themselves,</i>

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:52.778
<i>and that allows the males to spend</i>
<i>their whole time attracting females...</i>

00:26:53.487 --> 00:26:54.321
[cheeps]

00:26:54.404 --> 00:26:55.823
<i>...as manakins do.</i>

00:26:58.158 --> 00:27:00.452
<i>There are over 50 different species,</i>

00:27:00.536 --> 00:27:03.956
<i>each with its own</i>
<i>highly elaborate dance routine.</i>

00:27:06.041 --> 00:27:06.959
[cheeps]

00:27:11.672 --> 00:27:13.382
<i>The golden-collared manakin</i>

00:27:13.757 --> 00:27:16.260
<i>starts by clearing his dance floor.</i>

00:27:20.347 --> 00:27:21.181
[cheeps]

00:27:25.018 --> 00:27:28.188
<i>A female arrives</i>
<i>and he starts his routine,</i>

00:27:28.272 --> 00:27:30.566
<i>rocketing from one perch to another.</i>

00:27:30.649 --> 00:27:31.567
[fluttering]

00:27:32.734 --> 00:27:33.652
[cheeping]

00:27:35.112 --> 00:27:38.073
<i>She checks out every detail.</i>

00:27:47.249 --> 00:27:50.878
<i>Finally, he performs his signature move.</i>

00:27:51.712 --> 00:27:52.838
[tweets]

00:27:52.921 --> 00:27:54.298
<i>The back-flip...</i>

00:27:54.381 --> 00:27:55.215
[calls]

00:27:58.302 --> 00:27:59.511
<i>...with twist.</i>

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:03.974
[calls]

00:28:04.057 --> 00:28:04.975
<i>Perfection.</i>

00:28:06.435 --> 00:28:08.270
-[cheeps]
-[whir of wings]

00:28:09.104 --> 00:28:13.025
<i>The red-capped manakin</i>
<i>has a very different act.</i>

00:28:14.234 --> 00:28:15.152
[cheeps]

00:28:16.361 --> 00:28:18.697
<i>It's a kind of slither.</i>

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:19.656
[cheeps]

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:21.658
[feet pattering]

00:28:22.242 --> 00:28:23.493
[cheeps]

00:28:26.246 --> 00:28:27.080
[cheeps]

00:28:28.874 --> 00:28:30.626
<i>With wing snaps.</i>

00:28:30.709 --> 00:28:31.543
[snap]

00:28:31.627 --> 00:28:32.753
[calls]

00:28:35.589 --> 00:28:37.299
-[cheep]
-[snap]

00:28:37.382 --> 00:28:38.842
[wings snapping]

00:28:43.305 --> 00:28:45.682
<i>But it doesn't seem to be working.</i>

00:28:48.227 --> 00:28:49.061
[cheeps]

00:28:53.232 --> 00:28:54.066
[tweets]

00:28:59.905 --> 00:29:01.240
[wings snapping]

00:29:02.824 --> 00:29:04.326
<i>She's seen enough.</i>

00:29:07.788 --> 00:29:09.164
[chirruping]

00:29:09.248 --> 00:29:13.669
<i>The most complex routine</i>
<i>is that developed by the blue manakin.</i>

00:29:15.462 --> 00:29:19.967
<i>The lead male</i>
<i>is supported by three junior dancers.</i>

00:29:20.425 --> 00:29:22.511
[calling]

00:29:22.594 --> 00:29:25.305
<i>They practice together almost every day.</i>

00:29:28.141 --> 00:29:29.518
<i>During rehearsals,</i>

00:29:29.601 --> 00:29:33.647
<i>a young male in juvenile plumage</i>
<i>stands in for the female.</i>

00:29:33.814 --> 00:29:35.482
[twittering]

00:29:38.735 --> 00:29:41.697
<i>The dance</i>
<i>has to be perfectly synchronized.</i>

00:29:42.739 --> 00:29:44.533
[high-pitched tweets]

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:48.036
[chirrups]

00:29:48.203 --> 00:29:49.871
<i>With the lead male happy...</i>

00:29:53.208 --> 00:29:56.420
<i>they're ready to present their dance</i>
<i>to a female.</i>

00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:00.549
-[calling]
-[fluttering]

00:30:09.099 --> 00:30:13.520
<i>In a carousel of movements,</i>
<i>each male takes his turn at the front.</i>

00:30:14.396 --> 00:30:15.939
-[calling]
-[fluttering]

00:30:34.958 --> 00:30:37.794
<i>The lead male performs the final move.</i>

00:30:37.878 --> 00:30:39.421
[high-pitched tweets]

00:30:42.257 --> 00:30:43.508
<i>Have they done enough?</i>

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:46.720
<i>What's her decision?</i>

00:30:46.803 --> 00:30:49.014
[chirruping]

00:30:53.352 --> 00:30:54.269
<i>It's...</i>

00:30:54.728 --> 00:30:55.812
<i>a yes!</i>

00:31:00.359 --> 00:31:01.985
<i>A great team effort.</i>

00:31:02.319 --> 00:31:04.946
[chirruping]

00:31:07.574 --> 00:31:12.496
<i>Tropical forests cover</i>
<i>only seven percent of the planet's lands.</i>

00:31:14.414 --> 00:31:18.085
<i>Away from the tropics,</i>
<i>where the weather is seasonal and cooler,</i>

00:31:18.502 --> 00:31:19.753
<i>they're very different.</i>

00:31:23.382 --> 00:31:26.968
<i>The greatest of all is the boreal forest</i>

00:31:27.636 --> 00:31:31.348
<i>that extends right across North America</i>
<i>and Eurasia.</i>

00:31:38.438 --> 00:31:41.983
<i>It cannot grow</i>
<i>during the frigid grip of winter.</i>

00:31:46.279 --> 00:31:48.949
<i>The forests are a crucial refuge</i>

00:31:49.408 --> 00:31:53.787
<i>for the relatively few species</i>
<i>that are able to survive here.</i>

00:32:01.753 --> 00:32:03.171
[wind howls]

00:32:03.255 --> 00:32:04.673
<i>As winter approaches,</i>

00:32:05.132 --> 00:32:08.593
<i>caribou grazing on the open tundra</i>
<i>to the north</i>

00:32:09.052 --> 00:32:13.432
<i>head south to the forest</i>
<i>to seek food and shelter.</i>

00:32:25.026 --> 00:32:29.906
<i>Out here, temperatures may fall</i>
<i>below minus 40 degrees centigrade.</i>

00:32:38.457 --> 00:32:42.836
<i>The forest will give some protection</i>
<i>from the worst of the weather.</i>

00:33:08.862 --> 00:33:13.241
<i>But now the caribou</i>
<i>are not traveling alone.</i>

00:33:22.834 --> 00:33:23.710
<i>Wolves.</i>

00:33:24.878 --> 00:33:27.255
<i>They live in the forest year-round.</i>

00:33:32.761 --> 00:33:36.598
<i>And in the winter,</i>
<i>they specialize in hunting caribou.</i>

00:33:51.488 --> 00:33:53.907
<i>They must find the freshest tracks.</i>

00:34:05.502 --> 00:34:08.880
<i>They move fast by avoiding the deep snow,</i>

00:34:09.548 --> 00:34:13.134
<i>sticking to the hard-packed trails</i>
<i>made by the caribou.</i>

00:34:18.473 --> 00:34:21.142
<i>This wolf has found fresh scent.</i>

00:34:31.486 --> 00:34:33.321
<i>The caribou must be close.</i>

00:34:43.748 --> 00:34:48.420
<i>The herd chooses to stop to rest</i>
<i>on a frozen lake.</i>

00:34:50.755 --> 00:34:54.217
<i>Out in the open, they will be able to spot</i>
<i>approaching danger.</i>

00:34:54.301 --> 00:34:57.095
[faint grunting]

00:34:58.096 --> 00:35:01.266
<i>And sure enough, the wolves catch up.</i>

00:35:08.315 --> 00:35:10.567
[caribou bleating]

00:35:15.739 --> 00:35:17.949
<i>They start to test the caribou,</i>

00:35:18.867 --> 00:35:20.577
<i>probing for any weakness.</i>

00:35:21.494 --> 00:35:23.246
[rapid bleating]

00:35:33.632 --> 00:35:35.091
<i>Out on the open lake,</i>

00:35:36.051 --> 00:35:38.595
<i>the caribou can outrun the wolves...</i>

00:35:45.393 --> 00:35:48.355
<i>so the wolves drive them back </i>
<i>into the forest.</i>

00:35:56.571 --> 00:36:01.534
<i>Here, in the deep snow,</i>
<i>progress is much harder and slower.</i>

00:36:05.955 --> 00:36:07.499
[bleating]

00:36:10.669 --> 00:36:14.881
<i>And, hidden by the trees,</i>
<i>the wolves can get closer.</i>

00:36:20.679 --> 00:36:22.347
<i>The hunt is on.</i>

00:36:23.431 --> 00:36:24.974
[bleating]

00:36:26.351 --> 00:36:28.978
[snorting]

00:36:32.482 --> 00:36:34.859
[bleating]

00:36:34.943 --> 00:36:38.988
<i>The pack must decide</i>
<i>which particular caribou to target</i>

00:36:40.865 --> 00:36:42.409
<i>and which trail to take.</i>

00:36:49.666 --> 00:36:51.251
<i>As the caribou scatter,</i>

00:36:53.461 --> 00:36:56.214
<i>the leading wolf takes a wrong turn.</i>

00:36:59.968 --> 00:37:04.764
<i>It's a crucial mistake,</i>
<i>and the wolves abandon the chase.</i>

00:37:14.065 --> 00:37:18.403
<i>With the coming of spring,</i>
<i>the caribou will head north once more,</i>

00:37:18.653 --> 00:37:21.614
<i>leaving the wolves and the forest behind.</i>

00:37:24.617 --> 00:37:29.748
<i>They will travel 600 kilometers,</i>
<i>crossing mountains to reach the tundra,</i>

00:37:30.039 --> 00:37:32.876
<i>where the spring grass</i>
<i>will be sprouting again,</i>

00:37:33.418 --> 00:37:34.836
<i>and they can give birth.</i>

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:42.218
<i>But these migrations</i>
<i>are a shadow of what they once were.</i>

00:37:44.345 --> 00:37:48.308
<i>The herd has lost</i>
<i>nearly 70 percent of its numbers</i>

00:37:48.391 --> 00:37:50.268
<i>in the last 20 years.</i>

00:37:53.313 --> 00:37:58.151
<i>Their world and all of our planet</i>
<i>is now changing fast.</i>

00:38:05.033 --> 00:38:07.660
<i>At the furthest polar extremes</i>

00:38:08.286 --> 00:38:13.541
<i>lie the frozen wildernesses</i>
<i>of Antarctica and the Arctic.</i>

00:38:18.087 --> 00:38:21.174
<i>Though they may seem remote</i>
<i>to many of us,</i>

00:38:21.966 --> 00:38:27.388
<i>the stability of these icy wastes</i>
<i>is crucial to all life on the planet.</i>

00:38:33.186 --> 00:38:39.025
<i>But in just 70 years,</i>
<i>things have changed at a frightening pace.</i>

00:38:41.569 --> 00:38:46.449
<i>The polar regions are warming faster</i>
<i>than any other part of the planet.</i>

00:38:53.623 --> 00:38:57.710
<i>The Arctic in the north is a frozen ocean,</i>

00:38:59.671 --> 00:39:03.466
<i>and the sea ice,</i>
<i>on which all life here depends,</i>

00:39:04.509 --> 00:39:05.635
<i>is disappearing.</i>

00:39:13.184 --> 00:39:15.645
[panting]

00:39:21.526 --> 00:39:22.777
<i>Polar bears</i>

00:39:23.361 --> 00:39:27.407
<i>specialize in hunting seals</i>
<i>out on the frozen ocean.</i>

00:39:33.121 --> 00:39:38.042
<i>But that world is now, literally,</i>
<i>melting beneath their feet.</i>

00:39:42.338 --> 00:39:44.799
<i>The sea ice breaks up every year,</i>

00:39:45.341 --> 00:39:47.385
<i>but now this is happening earlier,</i>

00:39:47.719 --> 00:39:51.347
<i>and the bears' limited hunting season</i>
<i>is getting shorter.</i>

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:57.103
<i>This is already having a profound impact.</i>

00:40:01.024 --> 00:40:02.525
[gentle snorting]

00:40:09.532 --> 00:40:11.951
<i>Cubs are growing up underweight,</i>

00:40:14.162 --> 00:40:16.581
<i>which reduces their chances of survival.</i>

00:40:19.083 --> 00:40:20.585
[snuffling]

00:40:35.099 --> 00:40:37.644
<i>Within the lifetime of these cubs,</i>

00:40:37.727 --> 00:40:42.732
<i>the Arctic in summer</i>
<i>could be largely free of sea ice.</i>

00:40:45.568 --> 00:40:46.986
[gentle snorting]

00:40:58.831 --> 00:41:02.251
<i>It's not just the sea ice</i>
<i>that is vanishing.</i>

00:41:03.878 --> 00:41:08.174
<i>The ice that lies on land</i>
<i>is also changing fast.</i>

00:41:12.804 --> 00:41:14.847
<i>This is Greenland,</i>

00:41:15.682 --> 00:41:20.853
<i>a vast expanse of ice</i>
<i>one-fifth the size of the United States.</i>

00:41:28.069 --> 00:41:31.614
<i>This glacial ice,</i>
<i>together with the sea ice,</i>

00:41:31.906 --> 00:41:37.078
<i>protects our planet by reflecting</i>
<i>solar radiation away from the surface</i>

00:41:37.286 --> 00:41:40.289
<i>and so preventing the Earth</i>
<i>from overheating.</i>

00:41:45.878 --> 00:41:48.881
<i>But the Arctic is warming dramatically.</i>

00:41:56.431 --> 00:42:00.893
<i>The leading edge of the Store Glacier</i>
<i>may appear to be motionless,</i>

00:42:02.103 --> 00:42:06.107
<i>but glaciers can move</i>
<i>at up to 45 meters a day.</i>

00:42:06.232 --> 00:42:08.192
[ice rumbling]

00:42:13.072 --> 00:42:18.036
<i>Where this one meets the sea,</i>
<i>it towers 100 meters above the water,</i>

00:42:19.537 --> 00:42:22.832
<i>and continues downward</i>
<i>for another 400 meters</i>

00:42:22.915 --> 00:42:24.333
<i>beneath the surface.</i>

00:42:29.505 --> 00:42:31.382
[ice cracking]

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:42.143
[waves rushing]

00:43:07.877 --> 00:43:12.840
<i>Over the last 20 years,</i>
<i>Greenland has been losing ice.</i>

00:43:19.138 --> 00:43:22.767
<i>And the rate of loss is accelerating.</i>

00:43:25.186 --> 00:43:26.729
[ice rumbling]

00:43:31.943 --> 00:43:33.778
[crashing]

00:43:38.074 --> 00:43:41.452
<i>These massive icefalls</i>
<i>from the top of the glacier</i>

00:43:41.828 --> 00:43:43.371
<i>are just the beginnings</i>

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:45.790
<i>of a far greater event.</i>

00:43:47.458 --> 00:43:49.669
[icefalls crashing in distance]

00:43:50.586 --> 00:43:55.216
<i>A stretch of the front face of the glacier</i>
<i>over a kilometer long</i>

00:43:55.299 --> 00:43:57.343
<i>is starting to break away.</i>

00:43:57.426 --> 00:43:59.053
[ice rumbling]

00:44:01.889 --> 00:44:03.307
[ice crashing]

00:44:08.521 --> 00:44:11.190
<i>From 400 meters beneath the surface,</i>

00:44:11.732 --> 00:44:14.652
<i>the hidden ice is surging upwards.</i>

00:44:17.196 --> 00:44:18.823
[waves rushing]

00:44:30.877 --> 00:44:32.628
[crashing]

00:44:32.712 --> 00:44:36.591
<i>The breakaway of an iceberg</i>
<i>the size of a skyscraper</i>

00:44:37.633 --> 00:44:40.678
<i>generates a colossal tidal wave.</i>

00:44:42.263 --> 00:44:44.015
[wave rushing]

00:44:56.652 --> 00:44:58.905
-[crashing]
-[rumbling]

00:45:15.671 --> 00:45:17.673
[classical music score plays]

00:45:56.671 --> 00:46:02.885
<i>Within 20 minutes,</i>
<i>75 million tons of ice break free.</i>

00:46:10.101 --> 00:46:13.771
<i>Glaciers have always released ice</i>
<i>into the ocean,</i>

00:46:14.939 --> 00:46:18.192
<i>but now this is happening</i>
<i>nearly twice as fast</i>

00:46:18.276 --> 00:46:20.361
<i>as it did ten years ago.</i>

00:46:23.572 --> 00:46:29.120
<i>Around the world, ice is now feeding</i>
<i>vast amounts of fresh water into the sea,</i>

00:46:29.829 --> 00:46:35.626
<i>raising sea levels, changing salinity,</i>
<i>and disrupting ocean currents.</i>

00:46:39.005 --> 00:46:44.343
<i>Without the Humboldt Current,</i>
<i>the coast of Peru would fall silent.</i>

00:46:45.803 --> 00:46:48.889
<i>The seabird spectacle would be no more.</i>

00:47:00.526 --> 00:47:05.489
<i>All across our planet,</i>
<i>crucial connections are being disrupted.</i>

00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:14.665
<i>The stability that we</i>
<i>and all life relies upon is being lost.</i>

00:47:18.044 --> 00:47:21.088
<i>What we do in the next 20 years</i>

00:47:22.423 --> 00:47:26.635
<i>will determine the future</i>
<i>for all life on Earth.</i>

00:47:35.436 --> 00:47:40.566
<i>The rest of this series will explore</i>
<i>the planet's most important habitats,</i>

00:47:42.068 --> 00:47:45.780
<i>and celebrate the life they still support.</i>

00:47:53.371 --> 00:47:56.791
<i>We will reveal what must be preserved</i>

00:47:56.999 --> 00:48:03.672
<i>if we are to ensure a future</i>
<i>where humans and nature can thrive.</i>

00:48:13.808 --> 00:48:19.605
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com to find out</i>
<i>how our planet can thrive again.</i>

00:48:22.942 --> 00:48:24.693
[Ellie Goulding:
"In This Together"]

00:48:24.777 --> 00:48:29.240
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:48:31.534 --> 00:48:37.748
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world say</i>
<i>"It's now or never" ♪</i>

00:48:40.292 --> 00:48:44.713
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:48:44.797 --> 00:48:48.467
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:48:48.551 --> 00:48:54.598
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world say</i>
<i>"We're in this together" ♪</i>

00:48:54.682 --> 00:48:56.684
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>

00:48:57.059 --> 00:49:01.355
[vocalizing]

00:49:16.412 --> 00:49:18.247
Subtitle translation by: Metia Bethell